## Why This Is Asked
Testing offline-first sync across devices is a realistic challenge for consumer apps with data integrity concerns, multi-device usage, and eventual consistency.

## Key Concepts
- Offline-first architecture: Data is persisted locally and synced when connectivity is restored
- Conflict resolution strategy: Merge (combine changes) vs. Last-Write-Wins (most recent timestamp wins)
- Test isolation across devices: Each Playwright context maintains separate local storage and cookies
- Deterministic network shaping: Use Playwright's `route` or Cypress's `server` to intercept and delay requests

## Code Example
```javascript
const { test, expect } = require('@playwright/test');

test('offline-first sync with conflict resolution', async ({ browser }) => {
  // Create two isolated device contexts
  const ctxA = await browser.newContext();
  const ctxB = await browser.newContext();

  const pageA = await ctxA.newPage();
  const pageB = await ctxB.newPage();

  // Simulate offline on both devices
  await pageA.route('**/api/**', route => route.abort('failed'));
  await pageB.route('**/api/**', route => route.abort('failed'));

  // Device A creates a note offline
  await pageA.goto('/app');
  await pageA.fill('[data-testid="note-input"]', 'Note from A');
  await pageA.click('[data-testid="save"]');

  // Device B creates a note offline
  await pageB.goto('/app');
  await pageB.fill('[data-testid="note-input"]', 'Note from B');
  await pageB.click('[data-testid="save"]');

  // Restore connectivity
  await pageA.unrouteAll();
  await pageB.unrouteAll();

  // Wait for sync and verify
  await pageA.reload();
  await pageB.reload();

  await expect(pageA.locator('[data-testid="notes"]')).toContainText('Note from A');
  await expect(pageA.locator('[data-testid="notes"]')).toContainText('Note from B');
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you scale this to 5+ devices?
- How would you verify conflict resolution for concurrent edits to the same note?
- How would you test partial sync failures?

## Purpose

This question evaluates practical E2E testing skills, specifically around runtime feature flags, test data seeding, and UI assertions in a social application context.

## Key Concepts

- Feature flag rollout strategies
- Test data seeding for user cohorts
- Mocking remote config services
- UI assertions for different feature states
- Stability under mid-flight config changes

## Code Example

```javascript
const { test, expect } = require('@playwright/test');

test('feature-flag gated composer', async ({ page, context }) => {
  // Seed users and mock remote config
  const userA = await seedUser({ cohort: 0 });
  const userB = await seedUser({ cohort: 1 });

  await context.addCookies([
    { name: 'user_id', value: userA.id, domain: 'localhost' },
    { name: 'cohort', value: '0', domain: 'localhost' }
  ]);

  // Mock remote config for 10% rollout to cohort 1
  await page.route('**/remote-config', async (route) => {
    route.fulfill({ json: { composer_rollout: { cohort: 1, percentage: 10 } } });
  });

  // Verify cohort 0 sees old composer
  await page.goto('/home');
  await expect(page.locator('.old-composer')).toBeVisible();
  await expect(page.locator('.new-composer')).not.toBeVisible();

  // Post and verify
  await page.fill('.tweet-input', 'Tweet from cohort 0');
  await page.click('.submit-tweet');
  await expect(page.locator('.tweet:has-text("Tweet from cohort 0")')).toBeVisible();
});

test('mid-session config flip', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.addInitScript(() => {
    window.__INITIAL_CONFIG__ = { composer_rollout: { cohort: 1, percentage: 0 } };
  });

  await page.goto('/home');
  await expect(page.locator('.old-composer')).toBeVisible();

  // Flip config mid-session
  await page.addInitScript(() => {
    window.__REMOTE_CONFIG__ = { composer_rollout: { cohort: 1, percentage: 100 } };
  });
  await page.reload();

  await expect(page.locator('.new-composer')).toBeVisible();
});
```

## What Good Answers Demonstrate

- Understanding of Playwright fundamentals (page objects, context, routing)
- Knowledge of feature flag architecture and cohort-based rollouts
- Ability to mock external services for deterministic testing
- Consideration of edge cases like mid-flight config changes
- Clean, maintainable test structure with clear assertions

